  "p. 1 n. 1 (superscript after the end of the overline that runs over χeft ... ābtet in l. 1): Characters over which a line is printed are, in the papyrus, written in red."
 ],
 "notes": "Pages 1-2 read row by row from 'PLATE I.' l. 1 to the last row of p. 2 (ends '... neḥem en', l. 10, mid-sentence). Line numerals: 2 before t'eṭ-f (p1 row2), 3 before pesṭ, 4 before seśep-tu, 5 before pert (p1 row7); p2: 6 before t'eṭ-f, 7 before àri, 8 before àθi, 9 before ṭua-tu, 10 before en. Rubric overlines: p1 over ṭua (short line), one long line over χeft..ābtet, over àn (row2), t'eṭ-f, ànet'-ḥrà-k; p2 over t'eṭ-f only. No square brackets in the hieroglyph lines on pp. 1-2 (brackets only in the English). p. 1 row 7: a small raised mark like a superscript '1' right after the ba-bird; there is only one footnote on p. 1, so it may be a stray footnote reference or a stroke - not coded as a sign. Glosses for rā (p2 l.9) and final en (p2 l.10) have no separate English under them ('day ... every' spans ment rā neb; 'removed hath' spans neḥem en). Ḥeru-χuti gloss printed in braces over two lines."
